WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.935
[music playing]

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:04.537
It's the day
before the big cook,

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:09.342
and I've got one last very
important stop on the border

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:12.245
of the Great Smoky
Mountains is the National

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:17.083
Park that is inhabited
by the Cherokee Indians.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.719
I've heard that there is
a Cherokee dish that I

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:22.756
need to taste to believe.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:26.860
We are right in the
thick of the woods.

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:28.027
Malia, there you are.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:31.498
[speaking cherokee]

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.266
[speaking cherokee]
what does that mean?

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.802
It means, hello,
Gordon, and how are you?

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.070
I'm so happy to be here.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:39.606
Now Hominy?

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:40.573
Hominy.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.375
Hominy.

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:45.678
Is a very unique traditional
Native American food.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.782
It's basically corn on
steroids, and I'm finally

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:50.683
about to see how it's made.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.787
The process of turning
the corn to Hominy

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:57.724
just relies on this ash, this
hickory ash, and the corn,

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:58.658
and water and heat.

00:00:58.725 --> 00:00:59.759
Oh really?

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.194
Yeah, first off, if
you can finish getting

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.196
the rest of these
kernels off, and once

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:04.330
you get a few of them-- see?

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:05.432
You're looking like a pro.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:06.633
Gotcha.

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:08.034
And then we're going
to come over with our ash

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.269
and go over to the fire.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:10.770
That ash will turn
it black won't it?

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:13.740
It's the lye that's in the
ash that we need to remove

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:15.341
the coat off of the kernels.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.610
It makes it easier to digest.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.813
GORDON RAMSAY(VOICEOVER):
Now I know why there's

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.381
such a buzz around this dish.

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:25.752
There is lye in Hickory ash,
which is extremely corrosive.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:28.254
I'm excited to be shown
what this unique ingredients

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.490
will do to humble corn kernels.

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:34.260
And it also makes some more
amino acids available to you,

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:35.995
and you have less
vitamin deficiencies.

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.531
GORDON RAMSAY(VOICEOVER): It
makes sense that the Cherokee

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.301
developed a recipe that
would maximize nutrients,

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.471
but I'm not sold on
this dish just yet.

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.239
So does the ash
turn it bitter?

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:47.807
It doesn't turn
it bitter, but it

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.075
does give it a different taste.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:50.944
Yeah, it doesn't
look appetizing.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.379
Well, I'm sure
sometimes when you make

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.215
a dish it doesn't
look that appetizing,

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.616
until you're all
the way down right.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:58.184
GORDON RAMSAY(VOICEOVER):
Malia has

00:01:58.251 --> 00:01:59.919
obviously never seen me cook.

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:03.189
While we wait for this caustic
mixture to work its magic,

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.892
I learn about the traditional
way of grinding corn.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:06.893
Straight in.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:07.527
Yes.

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:08.495
yes.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:09.195
All right.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:09.963
Does that go in there?

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:10.597
You just--

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:11.164
Switch.

00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:12.031
Is that right?

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.199
Around, how?

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:14.801
What do you think?

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:17.337
Well it looks like the sort of
that ends too big for the hole,

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.172
but then that doesn't.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:20.173
Now let's try this.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.508
How many kids do you have?

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:22.942
Well no.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:25.478
Malia please.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:26.179
[beep] hell.

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:27.213
Honestly.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.481
I'll give you a little help.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:29.482
I'll give you a little help.

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:30.483
It won't go down the hole.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:31.251
Turn it around.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:32.151
Oh you do turn it around,

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.486
You do turn it around.

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:36.022
So the secret is not
to go too hard, right?

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:37.724
Right.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.726
Yeah.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:42.896
I'll have to teach you how
to say, help me in Cherokee.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:43.830
Yes, please.

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:45.131
[speaking cherokee]

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.633
[speaking cherokee]

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:47.734
[speaking cherokee]

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.170
[speaking cherokee]
It means help, and right

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.906
now I need a [beep] ton of it.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:55.441
So you are using a lot.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:57.443
Am I?

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.912
Look how slow you're going.

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.513
Work smarter, not harder.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.015
Yes, yes ma'am.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.082
Indigenous science.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:03.983
Gotcha.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:04.784
Slow and steady.

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:05.785
Here we go.

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.823
It doesn't feel right.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.091
[laughing]

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:13.426
GORDON RAMSAY(VOICEOVER):
I'm sure you'd

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:14.727
like to keep watching me
awkwardly poke around,

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:17.230
but thankfully it's
time to try our Hominy,

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:19.499
which has being thoroughly
rinsed and mixed with beans.

00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:22.368
That looks I mean it's
almost like a sort of French

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:25.405
Cassoulet, you know
like a bean stew.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:26.372
Yeah.

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:33.613
See lovely.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.248
I've been waiting
for that all day.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:36.182
That is delicious.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:37.517
Oh my God.

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.652
The corn and the beans
on a different level.

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:42.488
She elevated that dish into
something quite luxurious.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:46.492
That Cherokee history
is so prevalent today,

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.760
and I need to tap into that.

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:50.997
If I don't tap into
that, I'm dead meat.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:51.898
See ya.

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:52.832
Thank you.

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:58.438
[music playing]

